IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pha-offences`

-- Protection from Harassment Act 2014 (Singapore) -- Part 3, Division 1:
-- the civil actions and orders that arise from a contravention of Part 2.
--
-- ss 11, 11A, 12, 13, 13B and 14. Division 2 of Part 3 (ss 15 to 16BB, the
-- false-statement orders), Part 3A (the Protection from Harassment Court) and
-- Part 4 are not encoded; see NOTES.md.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules
-- skill. No pipeline, no independent test pass, no human gate.

§ `Protection from Harassment Act 2014 -- Part 3, Division 1`

§§ `Which sections feed which remedy`

-- This is where the Act is at its least symmetrical, and it is worth setting out
-- before anything else.
--
-- s 11(1): "The victim under SECTION 3, 4, 5 OR 7 may bring civil proceedings".
-- s 12(1): "the victim of an alleged contravention of SECTION 3, 4, 5, 6 OR 7
--   may make an application to a court for a protection order".
-- s 8A(1) and s 8B(1): the enhanced penalties are for "an offence under
--   SECTION 3, 4, 5 OR 7" (and under s 10 in respect of such a victim).
--
-- So s 6 -- abuse of a public servant or public service worker in the execution
-- of their duty -- supports a protection order and a subsequent-conviction
-- enhancement under s 8, and supports NEITHER the statutory tort NOR the
-- vulnerable-person or intimate-relationship enhancement. A bus driver abused at
-- work can stop it happening again and cannot be compensated for it under this
-- Act, and s 14 has abolished the common law tort that would otherwise have
-- answered.
GIVEN `the section` IS AN OffenceSection
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a contravention of this section founds the statutory tort under section 11` IF
    CONSIDER `the section`
    WHEN `section 3` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 4` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 5` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 7` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the section` IS AN OffenceSection
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a contravention of this section founds a protection order under section 12` IF
    CONSIDER `the section`
    WHEN `section 3` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 4` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 5` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 6` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 7` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `11 -- the statutory tort`

-- s 11(1): "The victim under section 3, 4, 5 or 7 may bring civil proceedings in
-- a court against any individual or entity alleged to have contravened that
-- section in relation to the victim".
-- s 11(2): "if the court is satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the
-- respondent HAS CONTRAVENED that section as alleged by the victim, the court
-- may award such damages in respect of the contravention as the court may,
-- having regard to all the circumstances of the case, think just and equitable."
--
-- Two things the subsection does not contain. There is no minimum, no cap and no
-- measure: "just and equitable" having regard to all the circumstances is the
-- whole of the guidance, and s 11A(3) shows the Minister can cap enhanced
-- damages but says nothing about ordinary ones. And there is no limitation
-- period of its own -- s 11A(3)(b) empowers the Minister to clarify how the
-- Limitation Act 1959 applies to a claim for ENHANCED damages, which leaves the
-- ordinary claim to the general law.
--
-- The operative word is CONTRAVENED, and that is what makes the next section
-- matter.
DECLARE TortClaim HAS
    `the section alleged` IS AN OffenceSection
    `the section was contravened in relation to the claimant` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the claimant is the person the respondent intended to harass under section 3` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the claimant is some other person harassed by a section 3 contravention` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the claim` IS A TortClaim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a claim lies under section 11` IF
        `a contravention of this section founds the statutory tort under section 11` (`the claim`'s `the section alleged`)
    AND `the claim`'s `the section was contravened in relation to the claimant`

§§ `11 read with the defences in Part 2 -- a drafting difference across five sections`

-- ss 3(3), 4(3), 5(3) and 6(4) each open: "In any proceedings FOR AN OFFENCE
-- UNDER SUBSECTION (2)" (or, in s 6, "for an offence under subsection (1) or
-- (1A)"), "it is a defence for the accused to prove ...". The prohibition is in
-- subsection (1); the offence is in subsection (2); the defence answers the
-- offence.
--
-- s 7 is drafted differently. s 7(2) -- the subsection that says when a person
-- unlawfully stalks another -- opens "SUBJECT TO SUBSECTION (7)". The defences
-- are therefore built into the definition of unlawful stalking, and so into
-- whether s 7(1) was contravened at all.
--
-- s 4(1A) is a third form again: "An individual or entity DOES NOT CONTRAVENE
-- subsection (1) where ..." -- a non-contravention, within the same section as
-- the s 4(3) defences.
--
-- Read literally, the consequence is that a respondent who proves their conduct
-- was reasonable has a complete answer to the offence under ss 3, 4, 5 and 6 and
-- no answer at all to the statutory tort, because s 11(2) asks only whether the
-- respondent "has contravened that section". Under s 7 the same proof defeats
-- both. And under s 4 a legitimate scientific or artistic purpose defeats both,
-- while reasonableness defeats only the prosecution.
--
-- THIS IS A READING OF THE WORDS AND NOT A SETTLED PROPOSITION. The contrary
-- argument is strong: a court may well read "contravened" in s 11(2) as meaning
-- conduct for which there is no answer, so that the Part 2 defences travel with
-- it. The encoding states the literal reading, names it as such, and asserts the
-- pairs so that the difference between the sections is visible rather than
-- assumed away.
GIVEN `the section` IS AN OffenceSection
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Part 2 defences are expressed as defences to the offence only` IF
    CONSIDER `the section`
    WHEN `section 3` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 4` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 5` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 6` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `section 7` THEN FALSE   -- s 7(2) is "subject to subsection (7)"
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- Stated as functions over the fact records so the cases can run the same facts
-- through both questions.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A Section4Facts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 4 respondent is liable in the statutory tort on the literal reading` IF
    `section 4(1) is contravened` `the facts`

GIVEN `the facts` IS A StalkingFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 7 respondent is liable in the statutory tort` IF
    `section 7(1) is contravened` `the facts`

§§ `11A -- enhanced damages for online activity`

-- s 11A(1): in proceedings for a statutory tort under s 11, "where the conduct
-- that is the subject of the claim comprises wholly or partly of any ONLINE
-- ACTIVITY, the court may, if it thinks just and equitable in the circumstances
-- to do so, award enhanced damages attributable to the online activity ...
-- where --
--   (a) the victim made a REASONABLE WRITTEN REQUEST to the respondent the
--       effect of which is to ask the respondent to address the online activity
--       that is the subject of the claim; and
--   (b) the respondent FAILED, WITHOUT REASONABLE EXCUSE, to address the online
--       activity WITHIN A REASONABLE TIME."
--
-- s 11A(2): the award "(a) is in addition to any general and special damages";
-- "(b) is DISTINCT from punitive damages or aggravated damages, and the
-- principles governing the award of those damages NEED NOT APPLY"; and "(c) may
-- in the court's discretion be awarded in addition to or IN LIEU OF any punitive
-- or aggravated damages."
--
-- s 11A(3): the Minister may prescribe maximum limits and clarify how the
-- Limitation Act 1959 applies. No regulations under this subsection were
-- retrieved; the section came into force on 29 June 2026.
--
-- So the practical gate is a written request. The victim who takes the post
-- down the legal route without first writing to ask gets ordinary damages only.
-- Both limbs are needed, and (b) has its own reasonableness escape for the
-- respondent.
DECLARE EnhancedDamagesFacts HAS
    `the conduct comprises wholly or partly of online activity` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the victim made a reasonable written request to address the online activity` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the respondent failed to address the online activity within a reasonable time` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the respondent had a reasonable excuse for the failure` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the claim` IS A TortClaim, `the facts` IS AN EnhancedDamagesFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may award enhanced damages under section 11A` IF
        `a claim lies under section 11` `the claim`
    AND `the facts`'s `the conduct comprises wholly or partly of online activity`
    AND `the facts`'s `the victim made a reasonable written request to address the online activity`
    AND `the facts`'s `the respondent failed to address the online activity within a reasonable time`
    AND NOT `the facts`'s `the respondent had a reasonable excuse for the failure`

-- s 11A(3)(a): there is at present no prescribed maximum, because no regulations
-- were retrieved. Recorded as a function so that a later change is visible.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a maximum limit on enhanced damages has been prescribed` IF
    FALSE

§§ `12 -- the protection order`

-- s 12(1): subject to s 12(9), "the victim of an alleged contravention of
-- section 3, 4, 5, 6 or 7 may make an application to a court for a protection
-- order."
-- s 12(2): the court may, "if it is just and equitable in the circumstances to
-- do so", make an order if satisfied on the balance of probabilities that --
--   (a) the respondent has contravened s 3, 4, 5, 6 or 7 in respect of the
--       victim; and
--   (b) "the respondent is LIKELY TO CONTINUE that contravention or to commit
--       another contravention of section 3, 4, 5, 6 or 7 in respect of the
--       victim."
--
-- Limb (b) is the gate that matters in practice. A single past contravention,
-- however serious, with no likelihood of repetition, supports a claim for
-- damages under s 11 and no protection order.
--
-- s 12(2A): the court "is DEEMED to be satisfied on the balance of probabilities"
-- of limb (a) if --
--   (a) the respondent has been convicted of that offence, or any offence
--       specified in the Schedule, in respect of the victim; or
--   (b) the court is satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the
--       respondent has VOLUNTARILY CAUSED HURT (Penal Code 1871 s 321) to the
--       victim.
--
-- Limb (b) of the deeming is a bridge from physical violence to this Act.
-- Causing hurt is not harassment and is not a contravention of ss 3 to 7, but a
-- respondent who has caused hurt is treated as having contravened them. The
-- Schedule in limb (a) does the same job through convictions: Penal Code hurt
-- offences (ss 323, 324, 325, 326, 327, 329, 330, 331, 332, 333) and
-- Private Security Industry Act 2007 s 17B(1).
DECLARE ProtectionOrderFacts HAS
    `the section alleged` IS AN OffenceSection
    `the respondent has contravened that section in respect of the victim` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the respondent has been convicted of that offence or a Schedule offence in respect of the victim` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the respondent has voluntarily caused hurt to the victim` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the respondent is likely to continue or to commit another contravention` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is just and equitable in the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the applicant is the person the respondent intended to harass under section 3` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the facts` IS A ProtectionOrderFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 12(2)(a) is satisfied` IF
        `the facts`'s `the respondent has contravened that section in respect of the victim`
    OR  `the facts`'s `the respondent has been convicted of that offence or a Schedule offence in respect of the victim`
    OR  `the facts`'s `the respondent has voluntarily caused hurt to the victim`

-- s 12(9): "For the purposes of THIS SECTION AND SECTION 13, the victim of any
-- contravention of SECTION 3 includes only the person to whom the respondent
-- INTENDED to cause harassment, alarm, distress or humiliation, and not any
-- other person harassed, alarmed, distressed or humiliated by that
-- contravention."
--
-- s 3(1) itself defines the victim as "the target person or any other person".
-- s 12(9) cuts that down for the protection order and the expedited order, and
-- for nothing else. s 11 has no equivalent.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A ProtectionOrderFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the applicant is a victim for the purposes of sections 12 and 13` IF
    CONSIDER `the facts`'s `the section alleged`
    WHEN `section 3` THEN `the facts`'s `the applicant is the person the respondent intended to harass under section 3`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the facts` IS A ProtectionOrderFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may make a protection order` IF
        `a contravention of this section founds a protection order under section 12` (`the facts`'s `the section alleged`)
    AND `the applicant is a victim for the purposes of sections 12 and 13` `the facts`
    AND `section 12(2)(a) is satisfied` `the facts`
    AND `the facts`'s `the respondent is likely to continue or to commit another contravention`
    AND `the facts`'s `it is just and equitable in the circumstances`

-- s 12(2B): what the order may provide --
--   (a) prohibit the respondent from doing any specified thing in relation to
--       the victim or a related person;
--   (b) where the likely contravention involves an offending communication,
--       require the respondent to stop publishing it, or not to publish anything
--       similar;
--   (c) refer the respondent, the victim or a related person to counselling or
--       mediation.
-- s 12(2C): limb (a) may grant the victim exclusive occupation of a shared
--   residence, "whether or not the shared residence is SOLELY OWNED OR LEASED BY
--   THE RESPONDENT".
-- s 12(2D): but the order "does not affect any title or interest" in the
--   residence.
-- s 12(2E) to (2G): orders against a third party, an internet intermediary and
--   an online location's administrator, each requiring the offending
--   communication to be stopped or access to it disabled.
-- s 12(10): "third party" excludes anyone likely to publish merely by providing
--   an internet intermediary service, a telecommunication service, public
--   internet access or a computing resource service -- so the carriers are
--   reached through (2F) and (2G), not (2E).
DECLARE OrderContent HAS
    `prohibits the respondent from specified things in relation to the victim` IS A BOOLEAN
    `requires the respondent to stop publishing the offending communication` IS A BOOLEAN
    `refers a person to counselling or mediation` IS A BOOLEAN
    `grants the victim exclusive occupation of the shared residence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the shared residence is solely owned or leased by the respondent` IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 12(2C) and (2D) read together: the respondent can be excluded from premises
-- they own outright, and the exclusion changes nothing about who owns them.
GIVEN `the content` IS AN OrderContent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order may exclude the respondent from premises the respondent owns` IF
    `the content`'s `grants the victim exclusive occupation of the shared residence`

GIVEN `the content` IS AN OrderContent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order affects the respondent's title to the residence` IF
    FALSE

-- s 10(1) backs every part of a protection order with an offence except the
-- s 12(2B)(c) referral to counselling or mediation. So the one obligation the
-- Act cannot enforce by prosecution is the therapeutic one.
GIVEN `the content` IS AN OrderContent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `every obligation in this order is backed by the offence in section 10` IF
    NOT `the content`'s `refers a person to counselling or mediation`

§§ `13 -- the expedited protection order`

-- s 13(1): on an application for a protection order under s 12(2), the court may
-- make an expedited order if satisfied that --
--   (a) there is PRIMA FACIE EVIDENCE that
--         (i) the respondent has contravened s 3, 4, 5, 6 or 7 in respect of the
--             victim;
--        (ii) the contravention is likely to continue, or the respondent is
--             likely to commit another IMMINENTLY; and
--       (iii) the contravention, if continued or committed, "is likely to have a
--             SUBSTANTIAL ADVERSE EFFECT on the victim or the victim's
--             day-to-day activities"; and
--   (b) it is just and equitable for the order to be made on an expedited basis.
--
-- Three differences from s 12: the standard is prima facie evidence rather than
-- the balance of probabilities; the future contravention must be imminent rather
-- than merely likely; and there is the extra requirement of a substantial
-- adverse effect, which s 12 does not have.
--
-- s 13(1A) mirrors s 12(2A)'s deeming at the prima facie standard.
-- s 13(1B) and (1C) allow expedited orders against a third party and an internet
-- intermediary, on the same pattern.
DECLARE ExpeditedFacts HAS
    `the section alleged` IS AN OffenceSection
    `prima facie evidence that the respondent has contravened that section` IS A BOOLEAN
    `prima facie evidence of a conviction for that offence or a Schedule offence in respect of the victim` IS A BOOLEAN
    `prima facie evidence that the respondent has voluntarily caused hurt to the victim` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contravention is likely to continue or another is likely imminently` IS A BOOLEAN
    `likely to have a substantial adverse effect on the victim or the victim's day-to-day activities` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is just and equitable for the order to be made on an expedited basis` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the applicant is the person the respondent intended to harass under section 3` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the facts` IS AN ExpeditedFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the applicant is a victim for the purposes of section 13` IF
    CONSIDER `the facts`'s `the section alleged`
    WHEN `section 3` THEN `the facts`'s `the applicant is the person the respondent intended to harass under section 3`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the facts` IS AN ExpeditedFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 13(1)(a)(i) is satisfied` IF
        `the facts`'s `prima facie evidence that the respondent has contravened that section`
    OR  `the facts`'s `prima facie evidence of a conviction for that offence or a Schedule offence in respect of the victim`
    OR  `the facts`'s `prima facie evidence that the respondent has voluntarily caused hurt to the victim`

GIVEN `the facts` IS AN ExpeditedFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may make an expedited protection order` IF
        `a contravention of this section founds a protection order under section 12` (`the facts`'s `the section alleged`)
    AND `the applicant is a victim for the purposes of section 13` `the facts`
    AND `section 13(1)(a)(i) is satisfied` `the facts`
    AND `the facts`'s `the contravention is likely to continue or another is likely imminently`
    AND `the facts`'s `likely to have a substantial adverse effect on the victim or the victim's day-to-day activities`
    AND `the facts`'s `it is just and equitable for the order to be made on an expedited basis`

§§ `13B -- the mandatory treatment order`

-- s 13B(1): "WHERE A COURT MAKES A PROTECTION ORDER UNDER SECTION 12(2), the
-- court may, on the application of the victim or on its own initiative, make a
-- mandatory treatment order requiring the respondent to undergo psychiatric
-- treatment for a period not exceeding 36 months."
-- s 13B(2): it may also require residence in a psychiatric institution for all
--   or part of that period.
-- s 13B(3): before making one the court must (a) have reasonable grounds to
--   believe the respondent is likely to be suffering from a psychiatric
--   condition and that it is likely to be a contributing factor for the
--   contravention, and (b) call for a formal assessment report by an appointed
--   psychiatrist.
--
-- The opening words tie it to s 12(2) and to nothing else. An expedited order
-- under s 13 cannot carry one, which is coherent -- it is made on prima facie
-- evidence -- but it means the fastest route to protection is also the one that
-- cannot reach the cause.
DECLARE TreatmentFacts HAS
    `a protection order has been made under section 12(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `only an expedited protection order under section 13 has been made` IS A BOOLEAN
    `reasonable grounds to believe the respondent is likely to be suffering from a psychiatric condition` IS A BOOLEAN
    `reasonable grounds to believe that condition is likely to be a contributing factor` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a formal assessment report by an appointed psychiatrist has been called for` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the period of treatment required, in months` IS A NUMBER

GIVEN `the facts` IS A TreatmentFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may make a mandatory treatment order` IF
        `the facts`'s `a protection order has been made under section 12(2)`
    AND `the facts`'s `reasonable grounds to believe the respondent is likely to be suffering from a psychiatric condition`
    AND `the facts`'s `reasonable grounds to believe that condition is likely to be a contributing factor`
    AND `the facts`'s `a formal assessment report by an appointed psychiatrist has been called for`
    AND `the facts`'s `the period of treatment required, in months` AT MOST 36

§§ `14 -- the common law tort is gone`

-- s 14(1): "The common law tort of harassment is, to avoid doubt, declared to be
-- abolished and NO CIVIL PROCEEDINGS MAY BE BROUGHT for the tort of harassment
-- EXCEPT UNDER THIS ACT."
-- s 14(2): nothing in (1) prevents proceedings "for any act or conduct which
-- occurred before 15 November 2014."
--
-- This is what gives the gaps in s 11 their bite. Where s 11 does not reach --
-- a contravention of s 6, or the public servant abused at work -- there is no
-- fallback in tort, only whatever other cause of action the facts happen to
-- support.
DECLARE HarassmentProceedings HAS
    `brought for the tort of harassment` IS A BOOLEAN
    `brought under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the act or conduct occurred before 15 November 2014` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the proceedings` IS A HarassmentProceedings
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the proceedings may be brought` IF
        NOT `the proceedings`'s `brought for the tort of harassment`
    OR  `the proceedings`'s `brought under this Act`
    OR  `the proceedings`'s `the act or conduct occurred before 15 November 2014`

§§ `The three asymmetries, stated as questions`

-- These functions answer nothing a litigant asks. They exist so that the gaps
-- between the remedies are written down and asserted, rather than left to be
-- rediscovered.

-- 1. The section 3 bystander. s 3(1) makes "any other person" caused harassment
-- a victim. s 11 lets the victim under s 3 sue. s 12(9) confines the victim for
-- the protection order to the intended target. So the bystander can recover
-- money and cannot stop it happening again.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a bystander harassed by a section 3 contravention may sue for damages` IF
    TRUE

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a bystander harassed by a section 3 contravention may obtain a protection order` IF
    FALSE

-- 2. The section 6 victim. A protection order, yes. The statutory tort, no. The
-- vulnerable-person and intimate-relationship enhancements, no. The common law
-- tort, abolished.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a public service worker abused at work may obtain a protection order` IF
    `a contravention of this section founds a protection order under section 12` `section 6`

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a public service worker abused at work may sue for damages under this Act` IF
    `a contravention of this section founds the statutory tort under section 11` `section 6`

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a public service worker abused at work may sue in the common law tort of harassment` IF
    FALSE

-- 3. The defences. Under ss 3, 4, 5 and 6 a proved reasonableness defence
-- answers the prosecution and, on the literal reading of s 11(2), not the
-- claim. Under s 7 it answers both.
GIVEN `the section` IS AN OffenceSection
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `proving the conduct was reasonable answers the statutory tort` IF
    NOT `the Part 2 defences are expressed as defences to the offence only` `the section`
